{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1392229-tips-for-making-the-scenario-for-a-one-shot-a-time-bubble","id":1392229,"title":"Tips for making the scenario for a one-shot: A time bubble.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-28T09:52:34-07:00","id":1392229,"title":"Tips for making the scenario for a one-shot: A time bubble.","content":"Hello, Im a begginer at mage. I've basically only read the core book of the second edition, so I might be lacking info, not to mention some concepts are still not totally clear to me.<br /> <br /> Recently I've reread the book Mort by Terry Pratchett and thought about using the same concept presented in the book.<br /> <br /> Basically Death (the entity) gets a apprentice, but the apprentice (mort) fucks up on the job and kills(actually collects) the wrong person. As a result reality is split, in one reality the right target was killed, in the alternate reality the target is still alive. But reality heals like a wound, as result the stronger reality keeps pushing the the frail alternate reality until it turns into a reality bubble, that shrinks more and more around the target. People can enter and leave this bubble, crossing realities, but they never notice or remember it, only beings with true vision, like mort, mages, or other beings can remember both realities after crossing the bubble. And basically the bubble keeps shrinking, until it fades away.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, can paradox actually works like this for the manipulation of time? Forming temporary times bubbles that shrink fast until they disappear? Because Im trying to make a one-shot into such a place. <br /> <br /> The game is a soft crossover (players are not the main creatures of each splat, but rather secondary beings, like revenants, hedge sorcerers, kinains, kinfolk, etc) I want the player to accidentaly enter the bubble and leaving. They later find cryptic notes written by themselves, alerting about not coming back to &quot;that&quot; place. <br /> <br /> I want to make the PCs return to the bubble, but the catch is that they realize that if they leave, they &quot;die&quot;. Their self that is there and aware that they are in a bubble. So They might want to create a work around to survive. Or even destroy the bubble from the inside, so their current consciousness survive.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The thing is, how could the players achieve this, and what other kind of people could be interested in the bubble? To help or antagonize the PCs? Technocracy could be interested in it? How they would act about it or a bunch of non-mages entering it? <br /> <br /> I want to use the bubble as the scenario for the one-shot, but Im not really sure on <i>how</i> to use it. Im just interested in the concept. Any tips on how to run this are appreciated<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T11:30:26-07:00","id":1392268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, can paradox actually works like this for the manipulation of time? Forming temporary times bubbles that shrink fast until they disappear? Because Im trying to make a one-shot into such a place. </div> </div> </div>Paradox can do a lot of things. It usually punishes the mage that caused it, not other people, but sufficently big paradoxes can alter local reality and involve other people.<br /> <br /> Your idea does resemble to Paradox Realms - bubbles of reality that float in the Umbra that act as a sort of prision for wizards. It also resembles somewhat to Wrinkle, a Paradox Spirit that can put mages in a time loop until they repent from altering reality and solve the situation w/o magick. So, it could be the doing of a Paradox Spirit or a yet uncategorized Paradox phenomena<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I want to make the PCs return to the bubble, but the catch is that they realize that if they leave, they &quot;die&quot;. Their self that is there and aware that they are in a bubble. So They might want to create a work around to survive. Or even destroy the bubble from the inside, so their current consciousness survive.<br /> <br /> The thing is, how could the players achieve this, and what other kind of people could be interested in the bubble? To help or antagonize the PCs? Technocracy could be interested in it? How they would act about it or a bunch of non-mages entering it? </div> </div> </div>In general, Paradox works a bit like a bomb and a bit like a puzzle. For instance, Paradox Realms place test on mages trapped inside them (that need to be solved without magick), and if the mage solves the problem they return to Earth. So...this is not a standard paradox, but you could use a similar approach: Something needs to be done to set Reality right, maybe discovering how the target was supposed to die, and killing it that way inside the bubble.<br /> <br /> And what other people could be interested: Anyone that studies magical phenomena - including, yeah, most mages (albeit most mages would avoid it as soon as they discover it's a form of Paradox). Some mages study Paradox, and could be interested in such a peculiar manifestation - a mage like that could be a great ally, or just see the characters as guinea pigs. This includes the Technocracy, but it's not limited to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T12:35:48-07:00","id":1392276,"title":"","content":"I love this idea! I am planning a one shot game this coming Monday, a way to introduce my group to the M20 rulles and see if they like it enough to start a campaign. The adventure I am adapting is &quot;Weighing the Cost&quot;, based on the short story &quot;Dabda&quot; from <u>Truth Beyond Paradox</u>. The story is solid, but not much of an intellectual challenge. It is an emotional drama, but that alone (imo) is not sufficient for a good Mage tale. The antagonist in question is a Marauder. A very sympathetic one, who went mad because of the death of his daughter.<br /> Long story short,: I am thinking that a better adventure/story would be to have the guy succeed at his goal of resurrecting his daughter with time magick, and a dangerous paradox bubble is the result. The players have to find a way to defuse it.<br /> But at what cost?!?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T13:42:23-07:00","id":1392299,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1392229-tips-for-making-the-scenario-for-a-one-shot-a-time-bubble?p=1392268#post1392268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In general, Paradox works a bit like a bomb and a bit like a puzzle. For instance, Paradox Realms place test on mages trapped inside them (that need to be solved without magick), and if the mage solves the problem they return to Earth. So...this is not a standard paradox, but you could use a similar approach: Something needs to be done to set Reality right, maybe discovering how the target was supposed to die, and killing it that way inside the bubble.<br /> <br /> And what other people could be interested: Anyone that studies magical phenomena - including, yeah, most mages (albeit most mages would avoid it as soon as they discover it's a form of Paradox). Some mages study Paradox, and could be interested in such a peculiar manifestation - a mage like that could be a great ally, or just see the characters as guinea pigs. This includes the Technocracy, but it's not limited to it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I was thinking more along the lines of &quot;the small city was destroyed by a disaster. A mage tried to use time magick to save the people he love, but then it is stuck on a loop inside a bubble. So, for the bubble to burst, they would need to<br /> <br /> 1 - Convince the mage to le it go and let the disaster happen<br /> <br /> or<br /> <br /> 2 - Saving the city (or at least the people) without magick? <br /> <br /> <br /> I also have some other questions... let's say the bubble was created in the 80s and the game is in the present time. After it burst would the city experience time as normal until they reach present time, even if for the players it all happens in a blino of a eye?<br /> <br /> And another question is, can the mage die, and leave behind this paradox anomaly, so the players have to correct the things themselves (if they don't to &quot;die&quot; in a certain way)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T13:46:53-07:00","id":1392302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1392229-tips-for-making-the-scenario-for-a-one-shot-a-time-bubble?p=1392276#post1392276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love this idea! I am planning a one shot game this coming Monday, a way to introduce my group to the M20 rulles and see if they like it enough to start a campaign. The adventure I am adapting is &quot;Weighing the Cost&quot;, based on the short story &quot;Dabda&quot; from <u>Truth Beyond Paradox</u>. The story is solid, but not much of an intellectual challenge. It is an emotional drama, but that alone (imo) is not sufficient for a good Mage tale. The antagonist in question is a Marauder. A very sympathetic one, who went mad because of the death of his daughter.<br /> Long story short,: I am thinking that a better adventure/story would be to have the guy succeed at his goal of resurrecting his daughter with time magick, and a dangerous paradox bubble is the result. The players have to find a way to defuse it.<br /> But at what cost?!?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I really loved the concept of history/reality healing itself. The book even states that in the end this time bubble is like a pearl, something bad that the main reality rejected, but that this small pearl of alternate reality might become a egg for a new reality once this one fades away. I really like the idea that alternate realities do not create a whole alternate universe and are instead actual physical bubbles, with hard limits that occupies a space.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-29T06:03:18-07:00","id":1392486,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rucun</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1392229-tips-for-making-the-scenario-for-a-one-shot-a-time-bubble?p=1392299#post1392299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking more along the lines of &quot;the small city was destroyed by a disaster. A mage tried to use time magick to save the people he love, but then it is stuck on a loop inside a bubble. So, for the bubble to burst, they would need to<br /> <br /> 1 - Convince the mage to le it go and let the disaster happen<br /> <br /> or<br /> <br /> 2 - Saving the city (or at least the people) without magick?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes.<br /> <br /> 1 it's most likely if these people were going to die naturally (esp. because they did die) - Figthing against this Reality was the act of Hubris that made the Paradox, and thus undoing it could cause the Paradox to go away (especially if this was caused by a paradox spirit wanting to teach a lesson).<br /> <br /> But both sound reasonable<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also have some other questions... let's say the bubble was created in the 80s and the game is in the present time. After it burst would the city experience time as normal until they reach present time, even if for the players it all happens in a blino of a eye? </div> </div> </div>Maybe?. With Paradox it's hard to know. It migth as well send each person involved to their respective timelines (with the wizard, if alive, being returned to the 80s to live it's life, and the characters to today). It's your call.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And another question is, can the mage die, and leave behind this paradox anomaly, so the players have to correct the things themselves (if they don't to &quot;die&quot; in a certain way) </div> </div> </div>If the Paradox it's related to the <i>effect</i>, it could remain 'til the effect itself erodes. It could also be that the mage &quot;loops&quot; back to being alive like nothing happened (I think that would be better in case 1)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-29-2020, 09:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-29T09:19:24-07:00","id":1392569,"title":"","content":"Thanks a lot for the answers!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-29T12:14:46-07:00","id":1392621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marko Markoko</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1392229-tips-for-making-the-scenario-for-a-one-shot-a-time-bubble?p=1392276#post1392276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love this idea! I am planning a one shot game this coming Monday, a way to introduce my group to the M20 rulles and see if they like it enough to start a campaign. The adventure I am adapting is &quot;Weighing the Cost&quot;, based on the short story &quot;Dabda&quot; from <u>Truth Beyond Paradox</u>. The story is solid, but not much of an intellectual challenge. It is an emotional drama, but that alone (imo) is not sufficient for a good Mage tale. The antagonist in question is a Marauder. A very sympathetic one, who went mad because of the death of his daughter.<br /> Long story short,: I am thinking that a better adventure/story would be to have the guy succeed at his goal of resurrecting his daughter with time magick, and a dangerous paradox bubble is the result. The players have to find a way to defuse it.<br /> But at what cost?!?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a good emotional setup. If the mage turned Marauder succeeds, at minimum, it would alter the life of his daughter and her friends present at the original tragic event.<br /> <br /> What would happen to his magical partner, McAlester? What would happen to Jeremy's family and the town?<br /> <br /> I used the adventure as the first non-prelude and 'gather info' adventure in my current campaign; it was the first one with the whole group of PCs.<br /> <br /> For the OP, I would look at what your players are looking to get out of Mage and have the Marauder's actions, like those of the Marauder in <i>Weighing the Cost</i>, be actions a PC might reasonably take and be justified in taking. It's a lesson in hubris and how the Marauder reflects them would let you get into what the PCs need to do to walk the tightrope between exerting their Will and remaining in society.<br /> <br /> Since it's Paradox, even if you have time go back in a quick flash, I'd think fractured visions of certain snippets of the time since the city was pulled out of the timestream would make the problem more chilling. Any PC with Time 1 could notice the anomalies. . .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}